// @check
// @error `as i32` propagates null out of the enclosing function, and there is none here — at module scope, use `as!` to trap or `as?` to take a nullable
// @error `as i32` propagates null, which the enclosing function cannot return (it returns i32) — use `as!` to trap instead, or `as?` to take the null here
// @hint redundant type annotation: `ff64` is inferred as `f64`
// A BARE `as` to an integer target PROPAGATES the `null` its failure produces (DECISIONS.md
// §"Numeric `as` to an INTEGER target is exact-or-fail under the trio"), so it is legal only
// where the enclosing function can return that `null`. Two places cannot, and each gets its
// own sentence rather than one generic refusal:
//
//   · MODULE SCOPE — there is no enclosing function at all, so "the enclosing function
//     cannot return it" would name a function that does not exist.
//   · a function whose return type does not carry `| null`.
//
// Both messages NAME THE TWO SPELLINGS THAT WORK, which is the point of refusing here rather
// than accepting and truncating: the author is one character away from saying which failure
// they meant. The legal spelling — a `| null` return — is `as-cast-exact-trio.vl`'s `widen`.
//
// The refusals come from `castPropagationFits`, the same ladder the UNION-operand `as` uses;
// only the propagated remainder differs (`null` here, the unwanted arms there).
function ff64(x: f64): f64 { x }

const atModuleScope: i32 = ff64(3.9) as i32
print(atModuleScope)

function intoNonNullable(d: f64): i32 {
  d as i32
}
print(intoNonNullable(3.9))
